WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.481
Parliamentary privilege abuse.

00:00:02.431 --> 00:00:04.534
Above the law by design.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:12.329
Parliamentary privilege is a centuries-old constitutional principle that protects the independence of Parliament from interference by the Crown and the courts.

00:00:12.329 --> 00:00:18.659
In Canada, it means that Members of Parliament can say anything in the House of Commons without being sued for defamation.

00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:22.352
It means that committee proceedings are protected from court review.

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:27.852
And it means that Parliament can, in theory, compel the production of any document from any source.

00:00:27.852 --> 00:00:31.420
In practice, privilege has become a shield for the powerful.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.704
MPs make allegations under privilege that they would never make outside the House because they would face defamation suits.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:41.011
Committee subpoenas are issued but rarely enforced.

00:00:41.011 --> 00:00:44.147
Cabinet confidence blocks document production.

00:00:44.147 --> 00:00:48.636
The government invokes privilege to protect itself rather than to protect Parliament.

00:00:48.636 --> 00:00:54.874
The four key parliamentary privileges and how each has been used as a shield rather than a sword for accountability.

00:00:54.874 --> 00:00:57.852
The documented pattern of privilege abuse.

00:00:57.852 --> 00:01:03.363
Five instances where parliamentary privilege was used to obstruct rather than enable accountability.

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:07.397
Parliamentary privilege was designed to protect Parliament from the Crown.

00:01:07.397 --> 00:01:10.181
It now protects the Crown from Parliament.

00:01:10.181 --> 00:01:14.545
Defamation immunity shields MPs from consequences for their words.

00:01:14.545 --> 00:01:18.840
Committee majority control shields the government from committee investigations.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.806
Cabinet confidence shields government decisions from public scrutiny.

00:01:22.806 --> 00:01:27.181
Prorogation and dissolution shield the government from accountability processes.

00:01:27.181 --> 00:01:31.045
Each privilege exists for a legitimate constitutional purpose.

00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:35.397
Each has been used to obstruct the accountability it was designed to enable.

00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:45.352
This is the legislative layer of the institutional capture documented across TENET5: the tools designed to hold power accountable have been captured by the power they were designed to constrain.
